        "title": "Determining the Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"
      },
      "question": {
        ":question/id": "81fb6273-5dee-5beb-bfc9-3f4b77ae324c",
        ":question/problem": "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\frac{5}{4}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\frac{27}{4}}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}$ and ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4}.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4},$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=\\frac{27}{4},$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}=3\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.$",
